%[text] # Singular Value Decomposition: Properties and Applications
%[text] This plain-text live script targets MATLAB R2025b and is designed to replace a slide deck on the singular value decomposition. It emphasizes the structure of $ U $ and $ V $, the meaning of the singular values, and a few applications that are more persuasive when they can be computed live.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%[text] - the factorization at a glance
%[text] - what the matrices U and V do
%[text] - key properties of U and V
%[text] - how U and V connect to eigenvectors
%[text] - what spaces the columns of U and V span
%[text] - singular values rank and energy
%[text] - application: least squares and the pseudoinverse
%[text] - application: image compression
%[text] - closing summary \
A = [3 1; 0 2];
[U,S,V] = svd(A);
%%
%[text] ## The Factorization at a Glance
%[text] The singular value decomposition writes any real matrix as $ A = U\\Sigma V^T $. The diagonal entries of $ \\Sigma $ are the singular values, the columns of $ V $ are the right singular vectors, and the columns of $ U $ are the left singular vectors.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ A = U\\Sigma V^T, \\qquad A^TA = V\\Sigma^2V^T, \\qquad AA^T = U\\Sigma^2U^T $
%[text] For a classroom presentation, this first section replaces several static slides by showing the matrices and the reconstruction immediately.
factorTable = table(["A"; "U"; "Sigma"; "V"], {A; U; S; V}, 'VariableNames', ["Matrix","Value"]) %[output:01777e58]
reconstructionError = norm(A - U*S*V','fro') %[output:794205b1]
% Display U, S, and V with decimal approximation (not rational)
Usym = vpa(sym(U,'d'),3);
Ssym = vpa(sym(S,'d'),3);
Vsym = vpa(sym(V,'d'),3);
disp('U (symbolic, decimal):'), disp(Usym) %[output:8121554f] %[output:81dd7f7b]
disp('S (symbolic):'), disp(Ssym) %[output:812a2573] %[output:2ac2443e]
disp('V (symbolic):'), disp(Vsym) %[output:80afc05e] %[output:72fd440c]
%%
%[text] ## What the Matrices U and V Do
%[text] A productive interpretation is that $ V^T $ chooses the input directions, $ \\Sigma $ stretches or shrinks along perpendicular axes, and $ U $ rotates or reflects the result into the output space.
%[text] The columns of $ V $ identify orthonormal directions in the domain, while the columns of $ U $ identify orthonormal directions in the codomain.
theta = linspace(0,2*pi,400);
unitCircle = [cos(theta); sin(theta)];
afterVt = V'*unitCircle;
afterSigma = S*afterVt;
afterU = U*afterSigma;
tiledlayout(2,2) %[output:0e599db6]
nexttile %[output:0e599db6]
plot(unitCircle(1,:),unitCircle(2,:),'k','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:0e599db6]
hold on %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,V(1,1),V(2,1),0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0.85 0.33 0.10]) %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,V(1,2),V(2,2),0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0 0.45 0.74]) %[output:0e599db6]
axis equal %[output:0e599db6]
grid on %[output:0e599db6]
title('Input with V directions') %[output:0e599db6]
xlabel('x_1') %[output:0e599db6]
ylabel('x_2') %[output:0e599db6]
nexttile %[output:0e599db6]
plot(afterVt(1,:),afterVt(2,:),'k','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:0e599db6]
hold on %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,1,0,0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0.85 0.33 0.10]) %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,0,1,0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0 0.45 0.74]) %[output:0e599db6]
axis equal %[output:0e599db6]
grid on %[output:0e599db6]
title('After V^T') %[output:0e599db6]
xlabel('z_1') %[output:0e599db6]
ylabel('z_2') %[output:0e599db6]
nexttile %[output:0e599db6]
plot(afterSigma(1,:),afterSigma(2,:),'k','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:0e599db6]
hold on %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,S(1,1),0,0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0.85 0.33 0.10]) %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,0,S(2,2),0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0 0.45 0.74]) %[output:0e599db6]
axis equal %[output:0e599db6]
grid on %[output:0e599db6]
title('After Sigma') %[output:0e599db6]
xlabel('w_1') %[output:0e599db6]
ylabel('w_2') %[output:0e599db6]
nexttile %[output:0e599db6]
plot(afterU(1,:),afterU(2,:),'k','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:0e599db6]
hold on %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,S(1,1)*U(1,1),S(1,1)*U(2,1),0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0.85 0.33 0.10]) %[output:0e599db6]
quiver(0,0,S(2,2)*U(1,2),S(2,2)*U(2,2),0,'LineWidth',1.5,'Color',[0 0.45 0.74]) %[output:0e599db6]
axis equal %[output:0e599db6]
grid on %[output:0e599db6]
title('Output with U directions') %[output:0e599db6]
xlabel('y_1') %[output:0e599db6]
ylabel('y_2') %[output:0e599db6]
%%
%[text] ## Key Properties of U and V
%[text] The two most important structural facts are that $ U $ and $ V $ are orthogonal matrices and that their columns form orthonormal bases. In practical terms, they preserve angles and lengths, up to floating-point rounding.
%[text] This is often where a slide deck becomes too abstract. In a live script, the numerical evidence is immediate.
x = [1.2; -0.7];
y = [-0.4; 1.5];
propertyTable = table(["||U^TU-I||_F"; "||V^TV-I||_F"; "||Ux||_2-||x||_2"; "||Vy||_2-||y||_2"; "u_1^Tu_2"; "v_1^Tv_2"], [norm(U'*U - eye(size(U,2)),'fro'); norm(V'*V - eye(size(V,2)),'fro'); norm(U*x,2) - norm(x,2); norm(V*y,2) - norm(y,2); U(:,1)'*U(:,2); V(:,1)'*V(:,2)], 'VariableNames', ["Quantity","Value"]) %[output:32f1cfae]
columnTable = table((1:size(U,2)).', U(:,1), U(:,2), V(:,1), V(:,2), 'VariableNames', ["Index","UColumn1","UColumn2","VColumn1","VColumn2"]) %[output:9a421c80]
%%
%[text] ## How U and V Connect to Eigenvectors
%[text] The right singular vectors are eigenvectors of $ A^TA $, and the left singular vectors are eigenvectors of $ AA^T $. The corresponding eigenvalues are the squared singular values.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ A^TAv\_i = \\sigma\_i^2 v\_i, \\qquad AA^Tu\_i = \\sigma\_i^2 u\_i $
ATA = A'*A;
AAT = A*A';
eigenvectorResiduals = table((1:size(V,2)).', vecnorm(ATA*V - V*(S.^2)).', vecnorm(AAT*U - U*(S.^2)).', 'VariableNames', ["Mode","ResidualForV","ResidualForU"]) %[output:3a69945e]
squaredSingularValues = diag(S).^2 %[output:2e907b7f]
%%
%[text] ## What Spaces the Columns of U and V Span
%[text] For a rank-$ r $ matrix $ A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} $, the first $ r $ columns of $ U $ span the column space $ \\mathcal{C}(A) $, and the first $ r $ columns of $ V $ span the row space $ \\mathcal{C}(A^T) $.
%[text] In the full SVD, the remaining columns complete orthonormal bases for the null spaces. The last $ m-r $ columns of $ U $ span the left null space $ \\mathcal{N}(A^T) $, and the last $ n-r $ columns of $ V $ span the null space $ \\mathcal{N}(A) $.
%[text] When the original matrix is not full rank, some singular values are zero. Those zero-singular-value directions are exactly the directions annihilated by $ A $, so they reveal the dimensions that the transformation loses.
D = [1 0 1; 2 1 3; 1 1 2; 0 1 1];
[UD,SD,VD] = svd(D);
rD = rank(D);
spanTable = table(["rank(D)"; "dimension of Col(D)"; "dimension of Row(D)"; "dimension of N(D)"; "dimension of N(D^T)"], [rD; rD; rD; size(D,2)-rD; size(D,1)-rD], 'VariableNames', ["Quantity","Value"]) %[output:9a6fd890]
subspaceResiduals = table(["Projection onto Col(D)"; "Projection onto Row(D)"; "D times null-space basis"; "D^T times left-null basis"], [norm(D - UD(:,1:rD)*(UD(:,1:rD)'*D),'fro'); norm(D - (D*VD(:,1:rD))*VD(:,1:rD)','fro'); norm(D*VD(:,rD+1:end),'fro'); norm(D'*UD(:,rD+1:end),'fro')], 'VariableNames', ["Check","Residual"]) %[output:47094181]
rankDeficientSingularValues = diag(SD) %[output:43d168f5]
%%
%[text] ## Singular Values, Rank, and Energy
%[text] Singular values measure how strongly the matrix stretches along the special directions chosen by $ V $. Large singular values represent dominant action; small ones signal directions that are suppressed.
%[text] The effective rank and the quality of low-rank approximations are both visible in the singular value decay.
B = [4 0 3; 1 -2 0; 2 1 1; 0 0 0.2];
[UB,SB,VB] = svd(B);
sB = diag(SB);
energyB = cumsum(sB.^2)/sum(sB.^2);
singularValueSummary = table((1:numel(sB)).', sB, energyB, 'VariableNames', ["Index","SingularValue","CumulativeEnergy"]) %[output:670d2e43]
stem(sB,'filled','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:530b2cb3]
grid on %[output:530b2cb3]
title('Singular value spectrum') %[output:530b2cb3]
xlabel('index') %[output:530b2cb3]
ylabel('sigma_i') %[output:530b2cb3]
%%
%[text] ## Application: Least Squares and the Pseudoinverse
%[text] One major application of the SVD is solving overdetermined systems. The pseudoinverse uses the singular values and singular vectors to produce the least-squares solution, even when the columns are poorly scaled or nearly dependent.
C = [1 1; 1 2; 1 3; 1 4];
d = [6; 5; 7; 10];
[UC,SC,VC] = svd(C,'econ');
pinvFromSVD = VC*diag(1./diag(SC))*UC';
xLeastSquares = pinvFromSVD*d;
xBackslash = C\d;
leastSquaresTable = table(["intercept"; "slope"; "||Cx-d||_2"; "||x_svd-x_backslash||_2"], [xLeastSquares(1); xLeastSquares(2); norm(C*xLeastSquares - d,2); norm(xLeastSquares - xBackslash,2)], 'VariableNames', ["Quantity","Value"]) %[output:25d9cd74]
%%
%[text] ## Application: Image Compression
%[text] A grayscale image is a matrix, so the SVD can compress it by keeping only the leading singular directions. This makes the low-rank approximation idea visible instead of purely symbolic.
%[text] The comparison below shows that acceptable visual quality may survive even when the stored rank is much smaller than the full image dimension.
imageData = im2double(imread('cameraman.tif'));
[Ui,Si,Vi] = svd(imageData);
imageRanks = [5 20 50 100];
imageErrors = zeros(size(imageRanks));
imageStorage = zeros(size(imageRanks));
tiledlayout(2,3) %[output:1d0caef5]
nexttile %[output:1d0caef5]
imagesc(imageData) %[output:1d0caef5]
axis image off %[output:1d0caef5]
colormap gray %[output:1d0caef5]
title('Original image') %[output:1d0caef5]
colorbar %[output:1d0caef5]
for k = 1:numel(imageRanks)
    approximation = lowRankApprox(Ui,Si,Vi,imageRanks(k));
    imageErrors(k) = norm(imageData - approximation,'fro')/norm(imageData,'fro');
    imageStorage(k) = imageRanks(k)*(size(imageData,1) + size(imageData,2) + 1)/numel(imageData);
    nexttile %[output:1d0caef5]
    imagesc(approximation)
    axis image off
    colormap gray
    title("Rank " + imageRanks(k))
    colorbar
end
nexttile %[output:1d0caef5]
plot(imageRanks,imageErrors,'o-','LineWidth',1.5) %[output:1d0caef5]
grid on %[output:1d0caef5]
title('Relative error versus rank') %[output:1d0caef5]
xlabel('rank') %[output:1d0caef5]
ylabel('||A-A_r||_F / ||A||_F') %[output:1d0caef5]
imageCompressionTable = table(imageRanks.', imageErrors.', imageStorage.', 'VariableNames', ["Rank","RelativeFrobeniusError","StorageFraction"]) %[output:2f3c560c]
%%
%[text] ## Closing Summary
%[text] The SVD separates a matrix into orthonormal input directions, scaling factors, and orthonormal output directions. The matrix $ V $ explains where the important directions come from, and $ U $ explains where those directions land after the transformation.
%[text] In a presentation setting, the main payoff is that properties which are static on slides become observable: orthogonality, norm preservation, eigenvector relationships, subspace structure, low-rank approximation, least-squares fitting, and compression.
%[text] 1. The columns of $ V $ are orthonormal directions in the domain.
%[text] 2. The columns of $ U $ are orthonormal directions in the codomain.
%[text] 3. The leading columns of $ V $ span the row space, and the leading columns of $ U $ span the column space.
%[text] 4. If $ A $ is rank deficient, trailing columns of $ V $ span the null space and trailing columns of $ U $ span the left null space.
%[text] 5. The singular values quantify stretching along those paired directions and support applications ranging from pseudoinverses to image compression \
function approximation = lowRankApprox(U,S,V,r)
approximation = U(:,1:r)*S(1:r,1:r)*V(:,1:r)';
end

